.TH MONOCA 8
.SH NAME
monoca \- publish the authorised identity set as a signed Merkle root
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B monoca
.B enroll
.I name pkhex exp
.br
.B monoca
.B revoke
.I name
.br
.B monoca
.B epoch
.br
.B monoca
.B root
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B monoca
maintains the set of identities a fleet trusts.  Each identity is a
leaf
.IP
.EX
blake2b("mono1\e0" || name || "\e0" || pk || exp)
.EE
.IP
where
.I pk
is the 32-byte EdDSA public key in hex and
.I exp
is a Unix time after which the identity is not valid (0 means never).
The leaves are sorted by hash and folded into a Merkle root, so a
holder of one leaf can prove membership with an inclusion path, and a
verifier can prove a key absent with its two neighbouring leaves.  No
sparse tree and no per-user signature are needed.
.PP
Each change re-sorts the leaves, computes the root and signs an epoch
record:
.IP
.EX
epoch   \fIn\fP
time    \fIunix seconds\fP
window  \fIseconds\fP
root    \fI64 hex chars\fP
sig     \fI128 hex chars\fP
.EE
.IP
The signature is EdDSA over
.IP
.EX
"epoch1" || \fIn\fP || \fItime\fP || \fIwindow\fP || root
.EE
.IP
with each integer 8 bytes big-endian, so an epoch signature cannot be
confused with an identity signature.
.PP
.B enroll
adds a leaf.  Re-enrolling a name replaces its previous leaf, so key
rotation removes the old key from the authorised set rather than
leaving both valid.
.B revoke
removes every leaf for
.IR name .
.B epoch
prints the signed record and
.B root
prints the current root in hex.
.PP
The record being replaced is retained as
.BI roots/ n\fR,
so a token minted in the previous epoch can still be checked during the
grace window.  A verifier accepts only the current or the previous
epoch, and only until
.IR time\ +\ window ;
without a current record it must fail closed rather than accept a
stale root.
.SH FILES
.TF /keydb/monoca/epoch
.TP
.B /keydb/monoca/leaves
authorised set, one
.I "name pkhex exp"
per line
.TP
.B /keydb/monoca/epoch
current signed record
.TP
.B /keydb/monoca/root
current root, for convenience
.TP
.B /keydb/monoca/roots/\fIn\fP
retained previous records
.TP
.B /keydb/mono-signer
EdDSA signer key (private half; root only)
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/monoca.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR mcheck (1),
.IR mono (2),
.IR monoauth (8),
.IR certify (8)
.SH BUGS
There is no 9P server yet: the records are files under
.BR /keydb/monoca ,
not a served file tree, and nothing re-signs on a heartbeat, so an
idle CA's epoch ages out of its window.  The signer is read from
.B /keydb/mono-signer
until
.I tpm9p
supplies a sealed key.
.PP
An epoch is a full tree rebuild, so enrolling many identities is linear
in the set size; that is adequate for a fleet and is the first thing to
replace with an incremental tree if it stops being so.
